WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:11.140
A Node.js service may encrypt sensitive fields, sign JWTs, verify webhooks, connect to cloud services, and authenticate to databases.

2
00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:17.080
At first, the keys live in a local .env file because the team is trying to move quickly.

3
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:26.700
Later, copies appear in CI/CD variables, Docker build contexts, developer machines, support tickets, logs, and emergency notes.

4
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:31.440
The algorithm may still be strong, but the key lifecycle has escaped control.

5
00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:38.840
Keys are high-value assets because they unlock data, prove identity, authorize actions, or validate trust.

6
00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.380
A symmetric encryption key can reveal protected records.

7
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.520
A JWT signing key can mint trusted claims.

8
00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:48.500
An HMAC key can validate integration messages.

9
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.720
A TLS private key can impersonate a service.

10
00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:54.380
A cloud credential can reach infrastructure.

11
00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:59.200
These values deserve more structure than a random string pasted into configuration.

12
00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:10.760
Key management includes generation, storage, access control, use, rotation, revocation, backup, recovery, monitoring, and destruction.

13
00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:20.300
It is not solved by one environment variable, one secret manager, one KMS call, one Node.js crypto API, or one snippet from a wiki.

14
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:27.440
Those tools can help, but only when the system defines ownership, scope, access, and response behavior.

15
00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:35.000
The goal is to make key use intentional, scoped, protected, observable, and recoverable.

16
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:43.420
The team should know what each key protects, who can use it, where it lives, how it is delivered to runtime, how it rotates, and what happens if it leaks.

17
00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:53.600
Strong cryptography fails quickly when keys are generated poorly, stored carelessly, reused across purposes, logged accidentally, or impossible to revoke.
